    shadow: 'The Siren provokes as much as she attracts. Rome painted Cleopatra as a foreign temptress who had stolen a Roman general, and after the defeat at Actium in 31 BC that propaganda hardened into history. The role is also a cage: Marilyn Monroe fought for years to be taken seriously while studios sold her as a dumb blonde. A Siren has to stay larger than life and slightly out of reach, which is exhausting and rarely survives age or familiarity. The people drawn to her want the fantasy, not the person, and they drift away when it fades.',
    honest: 'Take pleasure in presence: dress with joy, slow down, use your voice well, and let people feel the relief of play. Offer the escape openly, as a holiday from duty that both of you chose, not as a trap that costs one of you a life.',
    question: 'What would you do if you stopped being sensible for one night, and why does the thought pull so hard?',
    dark: 6, everyday: 4, kin: ['physical-lures', 'poeticize', 'reformed-rake'],
    offers: 'A holiday from responsibility. Most adults live inside duty and routine, and the Siren offers total pleasure with a thrill of danger: a place where the rules are suspended and nobody has to be sensible. She is the escape route drawn as a person.',
    faces: [
      { name: 'The spectacle', text: 'Cleopatra knew she was competing for attention with Rome itself, so she never appeared as an ordinary woman. She arrived as a goddess, in costume, on a stage of her own design. The Siren treats every entrance as theatre and makes her difference visible from across the room.' },
      { name: 'The voice', text: 'Greene singles out the voice as the Siren’s oldest weapon, true to the myth. It is low, slow and unhurried, suggesting intimacy and a mind with nowhere better to be. Marilyn Monroe’s breathy murmur did as much as her looks to make men feel she was speaking to them alone.' },
      { name: 'The body and the costume', text: 'The Siren adorns herself to stand out, choosing clothes, cosmetics and jewellery that heighten the senses rather than flatter good taste. She moves slowly and with composure, so that every gesture can be watched. The effect is deliberate and constant, never casual.' },
      { name: 'The goddess and the child', text: 'The strongest Sirens mix sexuality with something innocent. Monroe’s wide-eyed, childlike manner made her seem both available and in need of protection, a contradiction that disarmed men. Cleopatra could be the learned queen of many languages and the playful companion at dice in a single evening.' },
      { name: 'The danger', text: 'The Siren is never quite safe to be near. She hints at a world outside the law, at ruin as well as rapture, and the risk is part of the pull. Without it she would merely be beautiful, and beauty alone soon becomes ordinary.' },
    ],
